{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a blind peer review of a research paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Main Conference (via ACL Rolling Review). Your focus areas are **novelty** and **related work**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ARR/EMNLP)\n- **Soundness** (1-5, half-point increments): 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor (main claims not supported), 1=Major Issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially Interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): 5=Award, 4.5=Borderline Award, 4=Conference, 3.5=Borderline Conference, 3=Findings, 2.5=Borderline Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Positive, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Willing to defend, 1=Not my area\n- **Reproducibility** (1-5): 5=Easily, 4=Mostly, 3=With difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot\n\n## Paper Title\n\"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\n## Full Paper Content (9 pages)\n\n### Abstract\nTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear. We present a dual contribution: (1) a quality estimation (QE) framework showing that five confidence signals across four families reliably predict structured IE output quality (AUROC up to 0.82, Spearman ρ up to 0.44), enabling practical selective prediction; and (2) a diagnostic analysis revealing why these same signals universally fail at best-of-N selection. We identify a multi-causal failure mechanism: instance degeneracy (21–58% of instances produce identical outputs across N samples), logprob score compression (within-instance range 0.041 nats), and insufficient signal discriminability even when degeneracy is reduced. A trained verifier (logistic regression on 5 s