{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013t6nQvUrDVfUvn5px2sdJX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both independent reviews are in. Now conducting the roundtable synthesis (Steps 2-4).\n\n## Roundtable Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\n**R1 → R2 key points to consider:**\n- N=8 is critically unjustified vs literature standard of N=40+. This threatens the \"selection universally fails\" claim.\n- WNUT-17 is claimed as a dataset but only appears qualitatively — weakens \"3 datasets\" claim.\n- Missing non-degenerate subset analysis (framing docs mention +2.9pp, p=0.004 on non-tied instances).\n- LoRA rank=8 ablation is confounded with model capability.\n- No MBR or temperature ablation baselines.\n\n**R2 → R1 key points to consider:**\n- QE framework (Contribution A) is incremental over Nédellec & Xia (2026) who already showed consistency-accuracy correlation for IE.\n- Title \"Cannot\" overstates — trained verifier partially succeeds, so the impossibility framing is inaccurate.\n- Missing significant related work: reward models for non-math, Universal Self-Consistency, CRF-era uncertainty, broader test-time scaling (DeepSeek-R1).\n- Negative result for fine-tuned models on well-solved benchmarks is partially predictable.\n\n## Roundtable Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Points of agreement:**\n1. The QE-selection decoupling is a genuine conceptual contribution (both reviewers agree).\n2. Multi-causal diagnosis is the strongest part of the paper (both agree it's well-structured).\n3. N=8 without justification is a significant gap (R1 raised; R2 concurs this limits generality).\n4. Trained verifier experiment is constructive but preliminary (R1: \"oracle labels limit practical applicability\"; R2: \"too thin for its narrative weight\").\n5. Cross-model validation is solid (R1 emphasized; R2 agrees breadth is adequate).\n6. Title overstates the claim (R2 raised; R1's concerns about missing N-scaling reinforce this).\n\n**Disagreement on excitement:**\n- R1: 3.5 (borderline conference) — sees practical value in QE framework + diagnostic.\n- R2: 3.0 (findings) — sees QE as incremental over Nédellec & Xia, negative result partially predictable.\n- Resolution: The diagnostic multi-causal analysis goes substantially beyond Nédellec & Xia (who only showed correlation, not the failure mechanism), but the novelty gap is real for Contribution A in isolation. Converge to **3.0-3.5 range** — the paper's value is primarily diagnostic, which is more Findings than Main Conference material unless the missing experiments (N-scaling, full WNUT, MBR baseline) are added.\n\n**Disagreement on reproducibility:**\n- R1: 3.5 (missing LoRA training details, alignment implementation, CV split strategy).\n- R2: 4.0 (sufficient for most claims).\n- Resolution: R1's specifics are valid — missing hyperparameter details are real gaps. Converge to **3.5**.\n\nNow synthesizing the final report. Let me load the submission tool."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9785,"cache_read_input_tokens":55049,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9785},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"352b5339-5de3-432c-8453-cee5bd2f497d","uuid":"238b456d-3a82-4f19-bd84-8220b2a8a2cb"}
